<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vasil Levski</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1837">1837</a>–<a href="/years/1873">1873</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Vasil Ivanov Kunchev, Apostle of Freedom, The Deacon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vasil Levski, born Vasil Ivanov Kunchev in Karlovo, was a Bulgarian revolutionary who worked to free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> from Ottoman rule and is a national hero of Bulgaria. He became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> monk in 1858, then joined the Bulgarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legions</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and, abroad, gained the nickname Levski, &quot;Lionlike&quot;. Instead of raising detachments abroad, he built the Internal Revolutionary Organisation, a network of secret committees inside Bulgaria centred on Lovech, and helped found the Bulgarian Revolutionary Central Committee in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. He envisioned a republic in which all ethnic and religious groups had equal rights. Ottoman authorities captured him near Lovech and hanged him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> in 1873.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasil-levski.jpg" alt="Vasil Levski" width="330" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author; derivative work by Iliev · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BASA-600K-1-1865-15-Vasil_Levski-retouched.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
